{
  "dom_id": "step-q342592",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $-8$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=2$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=-8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
